				\begin{document}$${v}_{t}^{\theta }$$\end{document}vtθ to perform long-range, direction-dependent transport to reach the target region. A distinguishing property of flow matching, in contrast to diffusion models where the terminal distribution is determined by the noise schedule, is that p0 need not be a standard Gaussian: any distribution from which efficient sampling is possible suffices66. The velocity field is trained to transport whatever p0 is supplied toward p1, enabling per-sample prior selection and even mixed isotropic and anisotropic batches within a single training step. FLOWR.ROOT exploits this flexibility by constructing task-specific anisotropic Gaussian priors whose covariance encodes the spatial extent and directionality of the generation context.

